\title{dtlog: logging for data.table operations}
\description{
\code{dtlog} provides feedback about \code{data.table} operations. It redefines the
subsetting method \verb{[.data.table} as well as several functions exported by
\code{data.table}, so it should be loaded \strong{after} \code{data.table}, otherwise there
will be no output. A more explicit way to resolve namespace conflicts is to
use the \code{conflicted} package.
}
\details{
The operations themselves are never changed: \code{dtlog} only adds a message.
Modification by reference (\verb{:=}, \verb{set*()}), keys, indices, return values and
visibility all behave exactly as they do in \code{data.table}, and every argument
is evaluated exactly as often as \code{data.table} evaluates it, so an argument
written as an expression with a side effect still runs only once.
}
\section{Options}{

\describe{
\item{\code{dtlog.display}}{\code{NULL} (default) prints with \code{\link[=message]{message()}}. A list of
functions sends the output to each of them. An empty list turns logging
off, as does anything that is not a function, which is ignored.}
\item{\code{dtlog.detail}}{\code{"full"} (default) reports value level information
(types, unique values, share of \code{NA}, number of changed values).
\code{"compact"} only reports rows, columns and column names, and never
copies data.}
\item{\code{dtlog.log_from_packages}}{\code{FALSE} (default) only logs calls made
from the global environment, so that \code{data.table} calls inside other
packages stay silent.}
\item{\code{dtlog.table_max_unique}}{\code{20} (default). A column with this many
unique values or more is described by \code{\link[=dttable]{dttable()}} as possibly continuous
instead of having its values listed. \code{Inf} lists every column.}
